{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310682,"nonce":"KbYuKOYESq+1I7JVO2nS64GPsfo=","title":".rayzor caches at PROJECT ROOT and compiler/ root persist across builds and can hold stale references from in-flight workflow patches that ...","message":"When intermediate workflow attempts add/remove stdlib MIR wrappers, the project-level caches at:\n- `./.rayzor/` (project root)\n- `./compiler/.rayzor/` (compiler root)\n- Per-example `nue/examples/*/.rayzor/`\n- `tools/llama-diff/.rayzor/`\n- `examples/*/.rayzor/`\n\nretain compiled artifacts referencing symbols that no longer exist. After revert, the running binary loads from these caches and SIGSEGVs deep in JIT code with `EXC_BAD_ACCESS (code=1, address=0x0)` — null deref because the cached symbol table has stale entries.\n\n**Symptom**: tiny-transformer / llama-chat SIGSEGV at exit code 139, crash address in JIT-compiled region (not in rayzor binary), `lldb image lookup` returns nothing meaningful, `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` shows nothing.\n\n**Fix**: \n```bash\nfind . -name '.rayzor' -type d -exec rm -rf {} +\ncargo clean --release -p rayzor\ncargo build --release --bin rayzor\n```\n\n**When this matters**: after ANY workflow that adds/removes MIR wrappers or runtime symbols, even if the workflow auto-reverts, scrub before testing the baseline. Per-example BLADE clears miss the project-root + compiler-root caches.\n\nThis bit during this session's bmm-threading workflow (`wh3ruilml`): tiny-transformer SIGSEGV'd persistently even after full revert + full cargo rebuild. The patches were structurally correct; the cache was holding stale wrapper references from a mid-workflow intermediate state. Full scrub + clean rebuild = green.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_rayzor_cache_scrub` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}